# ======================================================================
# library.agg  --  the shipped DecL library
# ======================================================================
#
# Version: 1
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# ONE library. It replaces examples.agg (the old curated set),
# cookbook.agg (the feature walkthrough) and actuarial-severity-curves.agg
# (the severity reference), which were three files with overlapping
# purpose, three different naming conventions, and no machine-readable
# grouping. It is the default `build` knowledge base, the source the
# Quarto cookbook renders from, and the source the aggregate_api (aLL) SPA
# reads for its Examples dropdown.
#
# NAMES are descriptive and GLOBALLY UNIQUE across kinds, so `build('X')`
# and `build.recipe('X')` always mean the same entry and no `kind=`
# disambiguator is ever needed. The old single-letter filing prefixes
# (A., B., K. ...) are gone -- grouping is what tags{} is for. Names that
# lead with a citekey (Mack2003.Lognorm) keep it: that is a citation, not
# a filing code.
#
# THE TRAILER carries everything a tool reads. Comments are for humans
# only -- nothing in src/aggregate parses them (the lexer deletes them
# before the grammar ever sees a line).
#
#   note{...}       one-line description. The SPA dropdown blurb.
#   tags{...}       comma- and/or space-separated slugs; grouping and
#                   selection (build.discover(tags=...)).
#   hints{...}      key=value; build settings, caller-wins.
#   doc{{{...}}}    the long-form recipe: Problem / Solution / Discussion
#                   / Check, markdown with fenced python. Rendered by the
#                   cookbook and RUN by tests/test_library_recipes.py, so
#                   an entry documents and tests itself. Closing fence
#                   must be alone on its line.
#
# WRITING A doc -- two conventions that matter:
#
#   <<decl>>   NEVER retype the program in the Solution. Write
#
#                  ```python
#                  a = build('''<<decl>>''')
#                  ```
#
#              and the entry's own declaration is substituted in, rendered
#              canonically and WITHOUT its doc (note/tags/hints are kept --
#              hints change how the object builds). Two copies of the same
#              program in one statement would drift the first time either
#              was edited; this is the whole point. Because the substituted
#              form is canonical, it can differ cosmetically from what is
#              typed above (e.g. `50% po L xs A` renders `50% so L xs A`) --
#              that is normalisation, not a change.
#
#   ```python  Plain fences, NOT ```{python}. Quarto never sees these as
#              source cells: `recipe()` emits them at runtime through
#              IPython display, and OUR code (aggregate.recipe.Recipe.run)
#              executes them. A ```{python} fence is Quarto *source* syntax
#              and would render as a literal label here. Plain ```python
#              also keeps the block copy-pasteable and syntax-highlighted.
#              A fence tagged anything else (```text) is prose and is NOT
#              executed -- use it to show output.
#
# TAG VOCABULARY -- namespaced, and it never restates the object's TYPE.
#
# Type is what `kind` is for: build.discover(kind='sev') finds every severity
# object. A tag saying `severity` on a `sev` entry would be dead weight, and
# would invite "why does type appear twice, which do I use?". So every tag
# carries a namespace and answers a question `kind` cannot:
#
#   topic:X   what the recipe is ABOUT -- severity frequency aggregate
#             reinsurance pnl portfolio distortion bivariate bounds ruin
#             numerics. Independent of type: an `agg` entry demonstrating a
#             severity form is topic:severity, and that is not a restatement.
#   role:X    where it stands -- hero (landing page) intro (teaching order)
#             reference (a lookup table entry) paper (reproduces a published
#             result).
#   check:X   which invariant the doc's Check asserts -- reconciliation
#             scaling-sweep independent-oracle limiting-case round-trip
#             cross-object.
#   slow      the ONE bare tag, deliberately: it names a pytest marker
#             (tests/test_library_recipes.py maps it to @pytest.mark.slow),
#             not a property of the subject.
#
# Enforced by tests/test_agg_libraries.py -- a bare, un-namespaced tag fails.
#
#   build.discover(kind='sev')                        # by type
#   build.discover(tags='topic:severity')             # by subject
#   build.discover(kind='agg', tags='topic:severity') # both
#
# PLACEMENT: on a `port`, the trailer goes on the `port` header line --
# a trailer written after the last unit binds to that unit, because the
# portfolio's trailer slot closes before the units are read.
#
# HOW MUCH DOCUMENTATION: every entry carries a note{...} -- that is the
# norm, and it is all `discover` and the object dropdown need. A
# doc{{{...}}} is for the cookbook-worthy few: the entries the cookbook
# actually teaches from, where a Problem / Solution / Discussion / Check
# write-up earns its keep. So build.recipes.query('not doc') is a
# DIRECTORY of the library, not a backlog.
#
# Statements are one per line here; DecL is whitespace-insensitive, so
# reflow freely.
# ======================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared building blocks -- referenced by name from entries below.
# Builtin references resolve by sequential load, so these come first.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
dist DualDistortion dual 2.5 note{Dual-power distortion; the second leaf for the minimum below.} tags{topic:distortion};
dist PHDistortion ph 0.7 note{Proportional-hazard distortion (universally understood).} tags{topic:distortion, role:intro, check:limiting-case}
doc{{{
## Problem

You want to load expected loss into a price in a way that charges more for the
tail than for the body, and you want the loading to be a *property of the
measure* rather than a judgement applied case by case.

## Solution

```python
import numpy as np
g = build('''<<decl>>''')
s = np.linspace(0, 1, 11)
qd(pd.DataFrame({'s': s, 'g(s)': g.g(s)}).set_index('s'))
```

The curve is the clearer statement of the same thing -- it bows above the
diagonal everywhere, which *is* the loading:

```python
#| fig-cap: "PH distortion, rho = 0.7. Above the diagonal is the load."
g.plot()
```

## Discussion

The proportional-hazard distortion is `g(s) = s**rho` with `rho = 0.7 < 1`.
Applied to the survival function, it stretches small exceedance probabilities
upward -- a 1-in-100 event is priced as though it were more likely than it is --
and leaves certainty alone.

Concavity is what makes it a coherent risk measure: the premium it produces is
subadditive, so combining two books never costs more than pricing them apart.
`rho = 1` is the identity (pure expected loss, no load); smaller `rho` loads
more heavily.

## Check

```python
# a distortion is a probability-to-probability map: g(0)=0, g(1)=1
assert abs(g.g(0.0)) < 1e-12
assert abs(g.g(1.0) - 1.0) < 1e-12
# increasing, and CONCAVE (the coherence property)
s = np.linspace(0, 1, 201)
gs = g.g(s)
assert np.all(np.diff(gs) >= -1e-12)
assert np.all(np.diff(gs, 2) <= 1e-9)
# a load, never a discount: g(s) >= s everywhere
assert np.all(gs >= s - 1e-12)
```
}}};
sev UnitSeverity dsev [1] tags{topic:severity};

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Showcase -- realistic books
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
agg CatXOLTower 1.74 claims sev lognorm 8.501 cv 14.624 occurrence net of 90% po 200 xs 200 and 95% po 400 xs 400 poisson hints{bs=2;log2=16;normalize=False;} note{ILW model on US Cat, amounts in billions.} tags{role:hero, topic:reinsurance};
bivariate CopulaWindFlood 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson note{Gumbel-copula dependence between Wind and Flood.} tags{role:hero, topic:bivariate};
agg ExposureRating [100 200 1000 50] premium  at [.9 .85 .9 .8] lr [250 500 1000 2000] xs 0 sev lognorm 120 cv 12 occurrence ceded to 750 xs 750 mixed gamma .2 note{Exposure rating on a small portfolio with limits profile, amounts in 000s.} tags{role:hero, topic:aggregate};
agg LimitProfile [1000 2000 500] prem at [.8 .7 .5] lr [1000 2000 5000] xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson note{Premium x limit x loss-ratio profile -- implied claim counts computed per band.} tags{role:hero, topic:aggregate, check:reconciliation}
doc{{{
## Problem

You have the shape every pricing actuary already has: a table of premium by
limit band, each band with its own expected loss ratio. You want one aggregate
distribution for the whole book, with the bands correctly weighted -- not three
separate models you then have to add up by hand.

## Solution

```python
a = build('''<<decl>>''')
qd(a.summary_df)
```

## Discussion

The three vectors line up positionally: 1000 of premium at an 80% loss ratio
written at a 1000 limit, 2000 at 70% at a 2000 limit, 500 at 50% at a 5000
limit. One severity curve is written across all three bands, and the *limit* is
what differs -- so the severity is the same lognormal capped at three different
points.

The claim count is an **output**, not an input. `aggregate` knows the expected
loss in each band (premium x loss ratio) and the expected severity in that band
(the lognormal limited at that band's limit), so it derives the implied count
per band and adds them. That is the whole point of the exposure clause: you
give it what you actually have -- premium and a loss ratio -- and it solves for
the count rather than making you do it.

Read `a.n` back to see the implied total count, and `a.sev_m` for the
exposure-weighted average severity across the bands.

## Check

```python
# the aggregate mean IS the premium-weighted expected loss, exactly:
# 1000x0.8 + 2000x0.7 + 500x0.5 = 2450
assert abs(a.actual_m - 2450.0) < 1e-9
# and the FFT estimate agrees to grid accuracy
assert abs(a.est_m / a.actual_m - 1) < 1e-6
# E[A] = E[N] x E[X] still holds across the blended profile
assert abs(a.n * a.sev_m - a.actual_m) < 1e-6
```
}}};
port PropertyCasualty tags{role:hero, topic:portfolio} note{Portfolio of property and casualty risks, amounts in 000s.} agg Casualty 5000 premium at 90% lr 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 200 cv 8 mixed gamma .45 agg Property 4000 premium at 85% lr 10000 xs 0 sev lognorm 250 cv 12 mixed gamma .15;
agg TweedieBook 25 claims sev gamma 1 cv .05 poisson note{Tweedie - Poisson sum of gammas. Extreme case, severity has low CV.} tags{role:hero, topic:aggregate};
port TwoLineBook tags{role:hero, topic:portfolio} agg Property 80 claims  500 xs 0 sev lognorm  50 cv 1.2 poisson agg Casualty 20 claims 2000 xs 0 sev lognorm 250 cv 2.0 mixed gamma 0.4 note{Two-unit book: thin property + mixed-frequency casualty. Capital and price downstream.};

