agg AggregateStopLoss 100 claims 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 5000 xs 30000 note{Aggregate stop-loss -- net of an annual 5000-xs-30000 layer.} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg BasicXOL 15 claims 10000 xs 0 sev lognorm 1500 cv 1.0 occurrence net of 50% po 2500 xs 2500 and 90% po 5000 xs 5000 mixed gamma 0.25 note{Occurrence excess-of-loss program, net view.} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg CededLayer 1250 premium at 80% lr 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 80 cv 1.675 occurrence ceded to 125 xs 0 and 50% po 125 xs 125 and 20% po 250 xs 250 and 5% po 500 xs 500 poisson note{Layered occurrence reinsurance tower, ceded view.} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg OccurrenceXOL 15 claims 10000 xs 0 sev lognorm 1500 cv 1.0 occurrence net of 50% po 2500 xs 2500 and 90% po 5000 xs 5000 mixed gamma 0.25 note{Occurrence excess-of-loss program, net view.} tags{topic:reinsurance, check:reconciliation}
doc{{{
## Problem

You have a two-layer per-occurrence excess programme with partial placements,
and you want the *net* aggregate -- with gross and ceded still visible, so you
can see where the cession went instead of taking the net on faith.

## Solution

```python
a = build('''<<decl>>''')
qd(a.reins_summary_df)
```

## Discussion

`occurrence net of ...` applies the programme claim by claim, before the
frequency convolution -- which is what "per occurrence" means and why it cannot
be done by adjusting the aggregate afterwards.

`50% so 2500 xs 2500` cedes half of the 2500-excess-2500 layer, `90% so 5000 xs
5000` nine tenths of the layer above it; `and` chains them into one programme.

Note the program above is the **canonical** form. Partial placements can be
written `so` (*share of*) or `po` (*part of*) and with a percentage or an
absolute amount; all of them normalise to the `<pct>% so <limit> xs <attach>`
spelling you see here, which is why what you wrote and what the library shows
back can differ without anything having changed.

`reins_summary_df` is the exhibit that matters here: three stages (gross,
ceded, net) x three components (freq, sev, agg), with theoretic `EX` next to
the FFT `Est EX`. The frequency row is identical across the three views --
occurrence cover changes what each claim costs, never how many there are.

## Check

```python
# net + ceded = gross, on both severity and aggregate, to grid accuracy
est = a.reins_summary_df['Est EX'].xs('occ')
for component in ('sev', 'agg'):
    gross = est[('gross', component)]
    net = est[('net', component)]
    ceded = est[('ceded', component)]
    assert abs(net + ceded - gross) < 1e-6 * gross
# occurrence cover leaves the claim COUNT alone. Read this off the theoretic
# EX column: the gross frequency has no separate FFT estimate (Est EX is NaN
# there -- there is nothing to estimate, the count is an input).
ex = a.reins_summary_df['EX'].xs('occ')
assert abs(ex[('net', 'freq')] - ex[('gross', 'freq')]) < 1e-9
assert abs(ex[('ceded', 'freq')] - ex[('gross', 'freq')]) < 1e-9
```
}}};
agg ReinsuranceAggregateLayer 5 claims 120 xs 0 sev 120 * uniform poisson aggregate net of 100 xs 240 note{aggregate reinsurance} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg ReinsuranceLayered 1250 premium at 80% lr 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 80 cv 1.675 occurrence ceded to 125 xs 0 and 50% po 125 xs 125 and 20% po 250 xs 250 and 5% po 500 xs 500 poisson note{Layered occurrence reinsurance, ceded view.} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg ReinsuranceOccurrenceTower 5 claims 120 xs 0 sev 120 * uniform occurrence net of 50% so 25 xs 25 and 90% po 50 xs 50 poisson note{occurrence reinsurance; note over the top exposure} tags{topic:reinsurance};
agg ReinsuranceOccurrenceWithAggLimit 5 claims 120 xs 0 sev 120 * uniform occurrence ceded to 60 xs 60 poisson aggregate ceded to 180 xs 0 note{occurrence with an aggregate limit} tags{topic:reinsurance};

